"use strict";(self.webpackChunkspa_template=self.webpackChunkspa_template||[]).push([[328],{3328:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"created":"2017-09-17T16:05:00","modified":"2017-09-17T16:05:00","id":1036,"title":".Net Database Profiling","article":"<p>I have published <a href=\\"https://github.com/lski/Lski.Data.Profiler\\">Lski.Data.Profiler</a> on nuget.</p>\\n<p>While developing a web application using <a href=\\"https://stackexchange.github.io/Dapper/\\">Dapper.Net</a> I needed to debug some queries that were being sent to the database. All I really needed was to be able to look at what was being sent, in the console or a log file, but there didnt seem to be a free or simple way to do it in my application, without installing a complete monitoring solution or paying for a database specific profiler.</p>\\n<p>There is a great open source (MIT) project called <a href=\\"https://github.com/MiniProfiler\\">Miniprofiler</a> by Sam Saffron of Stack Overflow that was built to profile the queries, but it was designed to output it in various front end environments. But all I wanted to do was look at my console.</p>\\n<p>So I developed <a href=\\"https://github.com/lski/Lski.Data.Profiler\\">Lski.Data.Profiler</a>, where I extracted the underlying connection wrappers in Miniprofiler and adapted them to be used directly where needed.</p>\\n<h2 id=\\"example\\">Example</h2>\\n<p>The example below shows how to add basic profiling to a connection object. After that all queries are sent to the console.</p>\\n<pre><code class=\\"hljs language-cs\\"><span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// original code</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> connection = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> SqlConnection(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"your-connection-string\\"</span>);\\n<span class=\\"hljs-comment\\">// replace with this</span>\\n<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">var</span> connection = <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> ProfiledDbConnection(<span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> SqlConnection(<span class=\\"hljs-string\\">\\"your-connection-string\\"</span>), <span class=\\"hljs-keyword\\">new</span> SimpleProfiler(Console.WriteLine));</code></pre>\\n<h2 id=\\"install\\">Install</h2>\\n<p>The project is available via nuget:</p>\\n<p>cli:</p>\\n<pre><code>dotnet add package Lski.Data.Profiler</code></pre>\\n<p>Package Manager:</p>\\n<pre><code>Install-Package Lski.Data.Profiler</code></pre>\\n<h4 id=\\"notes\\">Notes</h4>\\n<p>The project is still early stages and I have only provided a very basic output, it does work fully as intended though. I do hope to add to the the <code>SimpleProfiler</code> in time. But it is easy to add your own use case, simply create a new class that implements the IDbProfiler interface and create the <code>ProfiledDbConnection</code> with that instead of the <code>SimpleProfiler</code> above.</p>\\n","showInResults":true,"tags":["c#","data"]}')}}]);